# SPEC GOLDEN — hand-derived from css-flexbox-1 §9.7 (resolve the flexible
# lengths) on a *vertical* main axis, before the code existed. Never
# regenerated. Every length here is vertical, so it goes through the line rule
# and not the cell rule: 160px is 10 lines, 96px is 6, 64px is 4, 48px and 32px
# are 3 and 2. Read as widths they would be 20, 12, 8, 6 and 4 cells, and every
# number below would be wrong — which is the point of testing them.
#
# Container 1 — a definite height with `flex: 1` and `flex: 2`.
#   `flex: 1` is `1 1 0` and `flex: 2` is `2 1 0`, so both bases are 0 lines and
#   both hypothetical sizes are §4.5's automatic minimum, the item's content
#   height of 1 line. The container's inner main size is its own `height`: 10.
#   §9.7 step 4 hands out all 10 lines by grow factor, 1:2, so 3.333… and
#   6.666…. Whole cells: floor to 3 and 6, and the one line rounding dropped
#   goes to the earliest item that lost a fraction. 4 + 6 = 10 exactly — the
#   integer-cell invariant holds on this axis too, and a column that grew to
#   fill leaves no blank row at its bottom.
#
# Container 2 — `height: auto`, so the container is as tall as its items and
#   there would be nothing to grow into (see `flex-column`) — except that
#   `min-height: 96px` says it is at least 6 lines. That is what puts free space
#   back, and it is why the container applies its own min/max height *before*
#   §9.7 rather than leaving it to the clamp every block gets afterwards. Two
#   `flex: 1` items over 6 lines: 3 each.
#
# Container 3 — shrinking, and a minimum that freezes.
#   Bases 4 and 2 lines in a container of 3: 3 lines of overflow to take back.
#   Shrinking is weighted by the *scaled* factor (shrink × base), so the two
#   scale as 4:2 and the first pass gives 4 − 3·(4/6) = 2 and 2 − 3·(2/6) = 1.
#   The second item's `min-height: 32px` is 2 lines, so 1 is a violation: it
#   freezes at 2, and the whole 3 lines of overflow are re-divided among what is
#   left — one item, which drops from 4 to 1 (its own automatic minimum, the
#   1-line height of its text, is exactly where it lands). 1 + 2 = 3.
#   A single pass would have stopped at 2 + 2 = 4 and overflowed the container.
#
# Every item's width is the container's 20-cell content width: `align-items` is
# unset, so `stretch`, and a column's cross axis is the horizontal one.
<html>  x=0 y=0 w=20 h=19
  <body>  x=0 y=0 w=20 h=19
    <div.col.tall> flex column  x=0 y=0 w=20 h=10
      <div.a>  x=0 y=0 w=20 h=4
        anonymous  x=0 y=0 w=20 h=1
          line  x=0 y=0 w=20 h=1
            #text "a"  x=0 y=0 w=1 h=1
      <div.b>  x=0 y=4 w=20 h=6
        anonymous  x=0 y=4 w=20 h=1
          line  x=0 y=4 w=20 h=1
            #text "b"  x=0 y=4 w=1 h=1
    <div.col.floor> flex column  x=0 y=10 w=20 h=6
      <div.a>  x=0 y=10 w=20 h=3
        anonymous  x=0 y=10 w=20 h=1
          line  x=0 y=10 w=20 h=1
            #text "c"  x=0 y=10 w=1 h=1
      <div.a>  x=0 y=13 w=20 h=3
        anonymous  x=0 y=13 w=20 h=1
          line  x=0 y=13 w=20 h=1
            #text "d"  x=0 y=13 w=1 h=1
    <div.col.short> flex column  x=0 y=16 w=20 h=3
      <div.p>  x=0 y=16 w=20 h=1
        anonymous  x=0 y=16 w=20 h=1
          line  x=0 y=16 w=20 h=1
            #text "p"  x=0 y=16 w=1 h=1
      <div.q>  x=0 y=17 w=20 h=2
        anonymous  x=0 y=17 w=20 h=1
          line  x=0 y=17 w=20 h=1
            #text "q"  x=0 y=17 w=1 h=1
